meta {
  name: Get VPAs By Driver
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/vpas/driver/{{driverId}}
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{accessToken}}
}

docs {
  All VPAs linked to a driver, newest first (`VPAResource.getVPAsByDriver`,
  `onboarding/controller/VPAResource.java:67`). It is the only VPA-listing endpoint that allows the
  DRIVER role. Allowed roles: VAHN_ADMIN, TORG_ADMIN, FUND_MANAGER, TRUCK_MANAGER,
  ACCOUNTANT, OPERATOR, DRIVER.

  Soft-deleted VPAs (`deletedAt` set) are excluded; deactivated ones are still returned, so check
  `status`. There's no ownership check on {{driverId}}: any caller with an allowed role gets the
  list for any driver ID, including a DRIVER asking for someone else's VPAs.

  To call it as a driver, run auth/send-otp + auth/verify-otp with the driver's phone first so
  {{accessToken}} is a DRIVER token, and set {{driverId}} to that driver's ID.

  Response is a bare JSON array of VpaDTO, with no CommonResponse `data` wrapper:

  [
    {
      "id": 12,
      "vpa": "driver@upi",
      "vpaAddress": "driver@upi",
      "name": "...",
      "status": "ACTIVE",
      "verified": true,
      "verificationStatus": "...",
      "relationType": "...",
      "isDefault": true,
      "driverId": 42,
      "driverName": "...",
      "torgId": 315,
      "createdAt": "2026-09-01T10:00:00"
    }
  ]

  (Illustrative shape built from `VpaDTO`, not captured from a live call.) Returns an empty array
  if the driver has no VPAs or doesn't exist, not a 404.
}
